{"parentUuid":"81ee3204-9342-46b0-b937-2de61d770981","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"155ddb7f-58ed-457d-9951-ed46749f88cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EeDn8aRuiuifdKHMiBgDZ3","type":"tool_result","content":"338a861 G1: the quit question, the client's dark theme, and a face with a bold","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"19286266-7f71-4b3f-8a02-48b2bcbc04d2","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:05:42.034Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"338a861 G1: the quit question, the client's dark theme, and a face with a bold","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/PLAN.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":961,"oldLines":7,"newStart":961,"newLines":7,"lines":[" | O | done | Added 2026-11-06 (D33). Takes over A8 step 7's dial per checkpoint. O1 (9c52028): a step is lights-on or dark; the hold timer is gone; a workflow is lights-on when every step has a person's gate, dim when one up to the merge does, dark when none does; the checkpoints fold into the steps they judged; ship, land, merge and deploy wait before they act; stored jobs and settings map onto the steps. O2 (b176981): a step the factory runs keeps what it reported as its artifact; every decision is a `decide` record, a person's with their name and the step, a dark step's as its dial's. O3 (22331e0): Settings shows each pipeline's steps, each a lights-on or dark button, and the level they make; `PUT /dials/{stage}` sets one; the presets are the three levels. Found live and fixed: `turns` still checked for the short pipeline's stages, so full-pipeline turns kept no usage (migration 2); dark decisions logged out of order. Exit, live (2026-11-06), on the short pipeline of `demo-local` with the local forge: with every step lights-on, `j459f405e` waited at plan, build and land and landed after three approvals; with every step dark, `j5ba1f381` and `j2ab6822e` landed with no person, `multiply`, `subtract` and `divide` are in `calc.py`; the audit log has `plan: approved by Ian Johnson` and the like for the first, and `plan`, `build`, `land: approved by its dark dial`, in order, for the last. Jobs kept at approve and review before O wait on plan and build. Not shown: the full pipeline, as this server grants `sandbox::*` only at plan and build, so scope's calls were refused; the full pipeline's grants are A8's. `eval_arms` still checks for the short stages, so an eval of a capability at a full stage would be refused. 203 tests. |"," | W | in progress | Added 2026-11-06 (D35). Builds on O's steps and dials. W1 (a0cf9e9): a workflow is a graph of steps linked by results, kept as versions, with the built-in pipelines as chains. W1b (ef68e86): a step is a skill, a prompt, a command or a Bastion stage. W2 (ebc266d): the Workflows page draws each workflow on a canvas; an admin drags a step, links two from a step's right edge, adds and removes steps and links, opens a step to set what it runs, its dial, note and results, and saves a version; a built-in can only be copied. W3 (917681c): New request picks a workflow, and the job page draws the job's workflow version with each step's state and each result as a document; a workflow job's current step is its node. W4 (0bcbb3d, 85a1bcf): eleven seeded workflows. Live (2026-11-06), in the page against the local server: the database seeded the eleven; Short was copied as `Short with design notes`, plan given a `design-notes` result and a note, its link to build removed and drawn again (which found and fixed a bug: the link's preview took the pointer's release), and version 2 saved; `j73499b5a` ran on it from New request, plan kept `design-notes`, build's brief carried it as `<result name=\"design-notes\">`, and the job page showed it as a document. Not shown: removing a step, because Short has no step to spare and the full pipelines need A8's grants; a viewer's read-only view, because `local` has only its owner; the land, which waits on this server's GitHub forge and `demo-local` has no `origin` remote. The window's webview stayed blank to screen capture, so the page was driven in Chrome with the window's server. |"," | S | in progress | Added 2026-11-06 (D27 to D30). Runs before A8 step 4. S0, spike N12, passes (docs/levels.md): the page follows a server's events over `fetch` with the token, reconnects with `Last-Event-ID` after a server restart, and a second stack runs beside the first with its own data. The window's CSP gained `connect-src` for `127.0.0.1`. S1, the split: `crates/bastion-server` holds the engine adapter, the store, the factory, the gate, the forge, evals, settings, the audit log, charter, spec, Doctor, the stack and the console; the app links it as a library and keeps its commands, profiles and the window. `tauri::async_runtime` became `bastion_server::rt`, one `tokio` runtime; the fakes sit behind a `fakes` feature. `scripts/check-seams.sh` now fails if the server depends on Tauri or the page on the server. 86 core, 64 server, 9 app tests (2181a86). S2, the API: each command's body moved into `bastion_server::api` (de6861c), and the Tauri commands call it until S3. A `Server` holds the engine, the machine, the stack, a data directory, the factory, and a `Bus` of numbered changes (`Change` in `bastion-protocol`, the latest 256 kept for a reconnect). `bastion_server::http` serves one route per function behind the bearer token, with CORS for the window's origins: 400 for a refused action, 409 for the stack's typed refusal, 503 while the factory waits for the engine, 401 without the token. `GET /events` streams the bus as server-sent events, replays after `Last-Event-ID`, and sends `resync` when it cannot. The `bastion-server` binary takes `--checkout`, `--data`, `--port` and `--engine`, keeps its token in `data/token` (mode 0600), starts or attaches to the stack, and serves on loopback. Live (2026-11-06) against the second stack: the server started the stack, opened the store on the third try once `database` registered, ran migration 1 through the worker (`user_version` 1, the seed's 5 constraints and 3 grants), refused a request with no token, answered `/engine`, `/jobs` and `/constraints`, pushed a change on `/events` when a preset was set, answered a stale `Last-Event-ID` with `resync`, refused a bad preset with 400, and stopped the stack through `/stack/stop`. 86 core, 77 server, 3 app tests (62ea1a5). S3, the view on the API: the page sends each server command over HTTP by a route table in `bastion-protocol` (a server test sends every row and fails on a 404 or 405), with the token from the window's `target` command, and follows `GET /events` with `fetch`, reconnecting with `Last-Event-ID`; a resync, a reconnect or the window's focus makes every page read again, the engine status included. The app keeps three commands (`profile`, `target`, `quit`), no longer links the server, and starts `bastion-server` in its own process group, or attaches to the one on port 49190. The page decides a quit: it asks only while the stack runs, and the server outlives the app. Live (2026-11-06), on the owner's stack after backing up `data/bastion.db`: the app started the local server, migration 1 ran through the `database` worker and kept the 15 jobs and 83 artifacts; the Jobs page showed them and the Engine page the link up; with the server killed the Link card said `Down: http://127.0.0.1:49190 does not answer.`, and after a restart the open page came back up with no reload; a relaunched app attached to the running server instead of starting another. Not confirmed: the quit question (after its close button, screenshots of the window came back blank), and ⌘Q quits without asking, as Tauri's Quit menu item exits with a code; a stale error stays on the stack card after an outage. The sidebar's server label is gone (D30). 86 core, 78 server, 3 app, 3 view, 1 protocol tests (8004bb8). S4, Bastion servers, built: the app keeps the servers the owner added in `servers.toml`, each token in the Keychain (`keyring`), with `local` always first; the page follows every server's events and calls any of them (`call_on`). The Jobs page lists every server's jobs, each row naming its server, and the filter offers each server and workspace that has jobs. New request picks a Bastion server, `local` by default, then one of its workspaces; opening a job makes its server the current one, and the job page names it. The pages about one server have a Bastion server picker at their top, and Settings lists the servers with add and remove. The `profile` command is gone from the page; `profiles.toml` only tells the app how to start `local`. 86 core, 78 server, 5 app, 3 view, 1 protocol tests (fe68813). Exit, live (2026-11-06), with `local` and a second Bastion server on a second stack on this machine: Two was added in Settings, its token went to the Keychain and not to `servers.toml`; New request on `local` with `demo-local` started `j7df1a6c2`, and New request with Two picked showed only Two's workspace and started `jcbf97e51`; the Jobs page listed both under their servers, and each reached Approve with one plan. Closing the window with the stack running asked the quit question (S3's open item), Keep it running quit the app with both servers and both stacks up, and the reopened app listed both jobs where they were. Killing only Two's server left its stack running, and the restarted server attached to it. Found, and open: the Jobs page does not say when a server cannot be reached, and keeps showing its last read; stopping the background task that ran Two's server took its stack down too, and the job waiting at Approve lost its VM (`sandbox not found` at stop), so a job that waits across a stack restart cannot go on; whether a plan turn survives a kill mid-turn is not shown, because the plan finished too fast to tell; the add-server form keeps its values after Add. Then the Jobs page's unreachable server: the page now marks a server whose event stream is down, names it above the list, keeps that server's jobs as last read and marks them unreachable, and still says why a reachable server's jobs did not read; checked live by killing only Two's server, which put up the note, and restarting it, which cleared the note and brought Two's job back with no reload. Next: A8 step 4. |","-| G | in progress | Added 2026-11-09 (D37). Runs next, before A9. G0, spike N14 (docs/levels.md): GTK 4 and `iced` measured on the same three screens; the owner chose iced and dropped the baseline. G1 started: `crates/bastion-desktop` on `iced` 0.14.0 is the window, the sidebar with the Lucide icons, D4's theme as `theme.rs`, and the server picker on the pages about one server. It starts or attaches to `local` and reads the added servers from the files and Keychain entries the Tauri app keeps, so both clients share them until G4. A command goes over HTTP by `bastion-protocol`'s route table, and each server's `GET /events` stream is an iced subscription that reconnects with `Last-Event-ID`. Live: the window attached to the running `local` server, listed `local` up and the added server `Two` down (nothing listens on 49191), showed a push from a workflow copy, and showed `local` down and up again across a server restart. Closing the window while the stack ran asked the quit question; with the stack stopped, it quit at once. The client's theme is a custom iced `Theme` from D4's tokens, so a widget with no style of its own reads light on dark, and its face is Helvetica Neue, as iced draws no bold from macOS's variable system face. `scripts/check-seams.sh` allows the client no Bastion crate but `bastion-protocol`. Found: iced 0.14 has no screen-reader support (§11). 9 client tests. |","+| G | in progress | Added 2026-11-09 (D37). Runs next, before A9. G0, spike N14 (docs/levels.md): GTK 4 and `iced` measured on the same three screens; the owner chose iced and dropped the baseline. G1 done: `crates/bastion-desktop` on `iced` 0.14.0 is the window, the sidebar with the Lucide icons, D4's theme as `theme.rs`, and the server picker on the pages about one server. It starts or attaches to `local` and reads the added servers from the files and Keychain entries the Tauri app keeps, so both clients share them until G4. A command goes over HTTP by `bastion-protocol`'s route table, and each server's `GET /events` stream is an iced subscription that reconnects with `Last-Event-ID`. Live: the window attached to the running `local` server, listed `local` up and the added server `Two` down (nothing listens on 49191), showed a push from a workflow copy, and showed `local` down and up again across a server restart. Closing the window while the stack ran asked the quit question; with the stack stopped, it quit at once. The client's theme is a custom iced `Theme` from D4's tokens, so a widget with no style of its own reads light on dark, and its face is Helvetica Neue, as iced draws no bold from macOS's variable system face. `scripts/check-seams.sh` allows the client no Bastion crate but `bastion-protocol`. Found: iced 0.14 has no screen-reader support (§11). 9 client tests. |"," | A9 to A13 | — | — |"," | Later (§10) | on hold | — |"," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/PLAN.md"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d1b77a73-4291-4e87-9a7c-e28386b576c6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"81ee3204-9342-46b0-b937-2de61d770981","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"19286266-7f71-4b3f-8a02-48b2bcbc04d2","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_01EeDn8aRuiuifdKHMiBgDZ3","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"25edcc63-2d52-4ef0-9ed1-31eee3b68d2e","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:05:47.659Z","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"25edcc63-2d52-4ef0-9ed1-31eee3b68d2e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgt2wsv3CBXzJrzEkjumH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"G1 is done, but **the stack is stopped, and I didn't stop it.** The compose log shows all 30 containers going down after the first quit dialog appeared on screen. I never clicked \"Stop the stack\", so my guess is that someone clicked it, but I can't confirm that. The audit log can't be read while the engine is down. I left the stack stopped, and I'll start it when you say.\n\n**Housekeeping you asked for:**\n- `cargo clean` freed 10.2 GiB.\n- `brew uninstall gtk4` worked, and Homebrew also removed the libraries it had pulled in.\n- After every CI step passed, I pushed `2e342b2..22f6f0b`. The GitHub CI run for that push was still running when I last checked. It is the first one that builds iced on Linux.\n\n**G1, the iced shell:**\n- **Quit question:** with the stack running, closing the window asked whether to keep it running. With the stack stopped, closing it quit at once.\n- **Two look fixes:**\n  - The app now has its own dark theme, so text with no color set reads light on dark. Before, the dialog title was dark on dark.\n  - The font is now Helvetica Neue. iced couldn't draw a real bold from macOS's system font, so bold text fell back to a monospace face.\n- **Seen live earlier:** the server list with `local` up and `Two` down, a server push reaching the window, and reconnecting after a server restart.\n\nThe last commit, `338a861`, is local and not pushed. Clippy and the client's 9 tests pass, and so does CodeScene. The plan marks G1 done.\n\nNext is G2, the pages, starting with Jobs, New request and the job page."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":444,"cache_read_input_tokens":433409,"output_tokens":550,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":444,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":550,"cache_read_input_tokens":433409,"cache_creation_input_tokens":444,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":444},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"72e6cfce-3d1a-4df6-8996-687930f09826","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cgt2wscC1rYPfc12u8nB2","type":"assistant","uuid":"92b64b9a-7272-4ef8-b86b-e08b8f629299","timestamp":"2026-11-09T21:05:47.658Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
